{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Graph requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]` is a valid coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** The citation is a fenced `cite` with commentary on an explicit function that has a body. That is the form the built-in allows. The commentary gives a substantive reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Why the function stays unexecuted:** `node_content` is a required `Graph` trait method (`crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#trait[Graph]/fn[node_content]`). In `StoredGraph` it is deliberately a panic guard, because the storage-only fixture must never read contents.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - The tests in `storage.rs` that use `StoredGraph` go through `node_path`, `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`, which delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage`.\n  - `Validator::verify` and `check` run `StoredReview`, whose `execute` records no content dependencies.\n  - The panic would fire only if graph content were read, so the guard is expected to stay at zero executions.\n- **Claim about other fixtures:** The commentary says other graph fixtures exercise content resolution. That holds. `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs#impl[Application/Graph]/fn[node_content]` and `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs#impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[node_content]` implement real reads that record which nodes were read, and `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#impl[Memory/Graph]/fn[node_content]` returns real contents.\n\nI found no gap.",
